---
title: Cellular automata
---
Random noise, with cell life rules applied to every tile again and again, until a natural-looking map comes out of it [[t:73-74 t:83-84 s:9]].

## The rule
```text
fill the map with random wall and floor
repeat for every pass, for all tiles inside the edge at once:
    walls = how many of the tile's eight neighbors are wall
    the tile becomes wall if walls is 0, or 5 or more
    otherwise it becomes floor
```

The talk gives the rule in three cases: no neighbors, a wall; one to four, empty; five or more, a wall [[t:80-82]]. The neighbors its demo counts are walls, and a tile’s own state plays no part in what it becomes [[c03:40-70 g]].

## Step through it
@@steps limit=5 mid=1

## Controls
@@control wall_percent
The chance each tile starts as wall [[c03:19-29 g]].
@@control iterations
How many times the rule is applied to the whole map [[c03:31-34 c03:56-70]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

The rule says nothing about joining the caves up [[own]]. A map has {{c03.regions.median}} regions at the median and never fewer than {{c03.regions.min}} [[run:c03.regions.median c03.regions.min]]. The map has no start and exit of its own, so its path is measured between [our probe’s](#our-definitions) two points, and on {{c03.path.nulls}} of 200 seeds they lie in different caves [[run:c03.path.nulls]].

